136
00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.620
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So the interledger communication problem.

137
00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:55.059
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): You've got two networks, and there are different types of ledgers. Now, let's say the left-hand side is a Besu network, and the right-hand side is a Hyro network.

138
00:19:55.610 --> 00:20:07.509
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Besu is, of course, Ethereum-based and as a data format. And takes Ethereum, or ETH as currency, and a HIRO network.

139
00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:12.910
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Speaks Protobuf as a data format, and takes HBAR as currency.

140
00:20:13.820 --> 00:20:21.419
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): There are different consensus mechanisms, and there's really no internal way to send data between the two ledgers.

141
00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:29.989
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So, we're having to solve all of those types of problems, and I'm sure many of you are familiar with different interledger solutions.

142
00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:34.020
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Or use them, and so there's a variety of approaches.

143
00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:37.979
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So, data format is one problem.

144
00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:45.180
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): getting the payment… For remote execution of the transactions that you send across is another.

145
00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:53.490
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Getting the… getting the transaction to actually send between the two ledgers, how do you do that?

146
00:20:54.300 --> 00:21:01.560
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And then translating trust between the ledgers. How do you get to trust the data that's being sent so you don't have fraud?

147
00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:11.400
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Finally, you want an architecture for your protocol that is scalable and extensible to multiple ledger types.

148
00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:19.720
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So the major approaches have been, build a third letter type.

149
00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:24.149
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): One that's designed for, interledger communication.

150
00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:29.280
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And you have, essentially, oracles that monitor the source chain on the left.

151
00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:34.359
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Submit data to the Interledger ledger.

152
00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:50.319
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And then, push state changes to the destination ledger on the right. And you deploy smart contracts on either side to help with the process. Use oracles to monitor chain state, and

153
00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:51.719
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So on and so forth.

154
00:21:53.320 --> 00:22:02.030
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): As has been recent in the news, this is very error-prone. The fellows that tend to build the Interledger solutions.

155
00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:07.860
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And the reliance on oracles, or oracles are a huge attack vector.

156
00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:14.259
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And not everybody does their due diligence in performing these things, so…

157
00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:20.380
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): You're diluting the trust model by adding yet another trustable entity in the middle.

158
00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:29.550
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And you're creating a greater attack surface for hijacking assets. So, I'm sure we've all heard of the Layer 0 exploits.

159
00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.420
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And there's other exploits in attacking oracles.

160
00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.760
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): So… This is one approach.

161
00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:45.320
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Another approach is to go straight for the oracles, take the ledger out of the middle, just monitor or change state.

162
00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:56.750
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): On the source ledger, generate a standardized report format, and then on the destination ledger, have a contract that accumulates reports.

163
00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:03.970
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And then, once you've reached the threshold number of independent reports, you can.

164
00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:07.380
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): You know, execute the transaction that you want on the remote ledger.

165
00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:14.830
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Again, this has failure scenarios where people can attack the oracles or emulate the oracles.

166
00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:16.760
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): And this has happened as well.

167
00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:25.909
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): This would be the Chainlink model. I'm not saying Chainlink was attacked, but just that oracles in general tend to be an attack vector, and you're relying on

168
00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:35.969
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): whoever is standing up the Oracle system to do their due diligence and have secure process in place, redundancy, and so on and so forth.

169
00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:51.900
Edward Wertz (Hashgraph): Let me get to like clients. Like clients are basically let's proxy the consensus trust layer from one network to another, verify that trust layer.

